        "finalMessage": "I fixed all four issues in `useTTDChatStorage.ts`. I couldn't re-lint the file: oxlint isn't installed and the project has no `node_modules`.\n\n**Issues 1 and 3 (initial-load effect):** I added `loadChats` to the dependency list, so it's now `[loadChats]`. The effect can now run again when `loadChats` changes, but those extra calls do nothing. `loadChats` returns early while a load is in progress or once chats have loaded, and both failure and success mark chats as loaded. So in practice it still loads once on mount.\n\n**Issues 2 and 4 (auto-save effect):** Adding `saveCurrentChat` as a dependency directly would change behavior. It's rebuilt whenever anything in `chatHistory` changes, so typing in the prompt would trigger a save on every keystroke. If `persistenceAdapter` isn't stable, it could also loop forever, because each save creates a new `savedChats` array. Instead:\n- `saveCurrentChat` is now called through `useEffectEvent` (React 19.2+; the project uses 19.3). It always calls the latest version but doesn't make the effect re-run.\n- The old dependency list held the message count and last message ID without the effect ever reading them, which is why the rule called them unnecessary. They're now combined into one value the effect reads: `autoSaveKey` is `null` while the last message is generating, and otherwise the message count and last message ID joined into a string. The effect depends only on `[autoSaveKey]` and saves when it isn't `null`.\n\nSo a save still happens on mount, when messages are added or removed, and when generation finishes. As before, nothing is saved while generating. The only difference: the old code also saved when the last message's `isGenerating` changed between `undefined` and `false` and nothing else changed. That case no longer triggers a save.\n\nI also added `useEffectEvent` to the `react` import. No other code changed.",
